A||||25||Spektrum-87GT||welding||||1||
Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||10-20-2001||07:26 PM||||i'm about to get an arc welder, but have never welded before. I've heard its pretty easy, just takes a lot of practice until you're good. anyone have any tips on arc welding?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||10-20-2001||08:01 PM||||Hey Spek!<BR>See if a local hi school has adult shop classes...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||10-20-2001||08:21 PM||||Yep, it's pretty hard to teach over the net.<BR>High enough amps and penetration are the keys, plus a steady wrist. A good welding hood and protection. They have hoods that you can see thru, then they darken as soon as an arc is struck. Pretty exp$$, but worth it once you learn how to weld.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektrum-87GT||10-20-2001||10:23 PM||||well, the company i work for builds aircraft carriers and teaches welding at night school. its one of the best welding classes in the world, only problem is... its around 3 months long and lasts over 2 hours three nights a week. that seems like a LOT of time for what i plan on doing(little projects) The welder i'm getting isnt a high powered crazy weld and the projects I plan on doing are fairly simple and small, so i figure i can just practice a little on some scrap metal or something until I get good at it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||10-21-2001||06:31 AM||||What type of welder are you getting, a stick or wire feed?<P>I picked up a pretty good used 240V Miller wirefeed about a year ago that uses the Argon/nitrogen gas and had no previous experience welding and did very well with it in a short time.<P><BR>I've heard the 110V wirefeeds with flux core wire are ok for thin stuff and don't cost too much.<P><BR>I use the .023 wire which is good up to about 1/4" max and thats pushing it(should use the .030 for that thick) but for what I do it's a good size.<P>If the class is free at work I would find the time to take it as the info and practise would be great.<P>Let us know what type your getting and we'll see what tips we can offer.<P><P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||10-21-2001||01:37 PM||||Wire feeds are hard to beat, as long as the feed rate works right, and you can keep burn backs into the nozzle tip from happening. They're great on clean metal, but heck to use on rusty or painted stuff. I really like em on stainless and alum.I use a stick on mild and hardened steel. Tack it together with 6011 and weld over with low hydrogen like 7018.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektrum-87GT||10-21-2001||02:29 PM||||well, i just got a standard duty arc welder. Its made by campbell hausfeld, seems like a good welder for the projects I'll be doing. I was thinking about getting a wire fed, but they're too expensive and i only have a few things I want to do. The welder I got is 70 amps, 115 volts, welds up to 1/8" steel on a single pass. Definitly not for insane welding, but perfect for now. thanks for all the help, cant wait to start practing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jake_Dragon||10-21-2001||02:40 PM||||If given the choice go with the wire feeder.<BR>But if you do get a stick buy some extra rods and go to town. Get some 6010 3/32 rods and go to town on some scrap. 6010 and 6011 is a little more forgiving on dirty metal. <BR>When you get comfortable with the 6010 go get some 7018 3/32 and practice, practice, practice. 6010 is great for tacks and spot welds, also great for closing any gaps you may have. When you’re done run over it with a grinder and do all your build up with 7018. 6010 is a quick cool rod, you don’t get the strength of the 7018 but it is alittle more forgiving. Don't let your heat get out of control and you will be fine. Just remember that as your rod gets shorter the heat will go up. Start your weld with a short weld, about 1-inch long, grind it smooth and then finish the weld, this will preheat your metal and you won’t have to start too hot. <BR>Good luck Jake<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||AusFiero||10-21-2001||05:29 PM||||Welding at home without experience=The art of making your car unsafe to drive while at the same time creating fires in your garage you never thought possible. J/K<BR>I am considering buying  a MIG welder as I have heard they are really easy to use. Anyone got 1?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero2.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000009||Spektrum-87GT||10-21-2001||05:36 PM||||where do i get some scrap metal!? ive been looking around, but cant find anything that looks like it can be welded. i've heard(and read) you cant weld galvanized metal... and i went down to home depot to get some metal to practice on and everything was galvanized!! i ended up getting some painted black iron and will practice on that(sanding it first of course).<p>[This message has been edited by Spektrum-87GT (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||10-21-2001||05:44 PM||||You can weld galvanized sheetmetal, if you like cyanide  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Lovely blue smoke.... Tastes like burning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000011||maryjane||10-21-2001||06:13 PM||||sure you can weld galvanize. Make sure you have plenty of milk to drink afterwards. It WILL make you sick. But before you start, make sure you are up on safety stuff. There are plenty of tips concerning this on Licoln's website. Not saying you don't know what your doing, but I hate it when I burn my eyes. It's the only time I can see colors tho. Color blind the rest of the time.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||10-21-2001||06:52 PM||||LMAO.."Tastes like buuuuurning." - Ralph Wiggum<P>I agree that if you have any ability to get a wire feed, go for it.  The cheapest of wire feed welders will be better than the best arc welder for somebody without experience.  Some experienced welders claim they can arc weld as good as any wire feed, but for us beginners, why even bother? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Like these guys said, galvanized emits a very nasty smoke when you weld it.  You can use it, but I recommend you use a wire wheel or grinder and get all the coating off of the areas to be welded.  That, and perhaps wear a gas mask!<P>I know there was a junk-yard back home that also had scrap metal.  The best place to get it, though, was the scrap yard.  They had killer deals on sheet, rod, diamondplate, you name it.  Their stuff was still usable, and about half the price as new.  Call around and I'm sure you'll find a good place.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||10-21-2001||08:17 PM||||I can't remember... is it cyan blue smoke, or is it a thick yellow? It's been a while... That, and my memory is a little hazy. I DO remember hitting the ground, though...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000014||AkursedX||10-21-2001||09:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B><BR>I am considering buying  a MIG welder as I have heard they are really easy to use. Anyone got 1?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got a MIG welder.  My father does most of the welding in the family, but I did get to do most of the welding on the spaceframe of my 87GT.  It's pretty easy once you get the hang of it.  Although i did burn a couple holes where i shouldn't have, lol, but i fixed 'em.  It's wire-fed as well and i think those are alot easier to use.<P>If i remember, I'll post the pics of the welds that I did tomorrow.  <P><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Nashco||10-21-2001||09:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>I can't remember... is it cyan blue smoke, or is it a thick yellow? It's been a while... That, and my memory is a little hazy. I DO remember hitting the ground, though...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I seem to recall welding galvanized stuff made a thick yellow smoke.  Smells real real bad, and hurts if you take a deep breath.  Yeah...I got a fan real quick after taking a whif of that stuff!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||maryjane||10-21-2001||09:42 PM||||Made me sick for 12 hrs, an old welder admonished me to drink lots of milk to cure it. Since welding is up, I'll ask this: Anybody hear anything about welding with contacts in and the retina of your eye pulls away when you remove the contacts? Been a long time since I read it and can't remember where.||||reg||1
